WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.675
Idea. A T-shirt printer pays rent for its workshop, and buys a blank shirt for every T-shirt it prints.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:17.704
Rent is five hundred dollars at any output. Blank shirts cost four dollars each. Add them for total cost. Even at zero output, the rent must be paid.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.782
So total cost rises as output rises, but never falls below the fixed cost.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:39.968
Method. Steps one and two. Work out total variable cost: cost per unit times units. Then add the fixed cost. For one hundred and fifty shirts: six hundred dollars plus five hundred is one thousand one hundred dollars.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.483
If total cost is given, subtract instead. Fixed cost is total cost minus variable cost.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:56.157
Steps three and four. Write it in one line, fixed cost plus cost per unit times quantity, and give the answer with currency.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:15.923
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2021. A firm has fixed costs of fifty thousand dollars a month and variable costs of two hundred and fifty dollars per unit. It makes seven hundred and fifty units a month. Calculate total costs per month. Two marks.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.161
Pick out the three figures: the fixed cost, the cost per unit, and the output.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:31.052
Total variable cost is two hundred and fifty times seven hundred and fifty, which is one hundred and eighty-seven thousand five hundred dollars.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:46.551
Add the fixed cost. Fifty thousand plus one hundred and eighty-seven thousand five hundred is two hundred and thirty-seven thousand five hundred dollars. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.151
The variable part is much bigger here. Together they make the total.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:59.618
Common mistake. A common mistake is adding the cost per unit to the fixed cost without multiplying by output.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.365
Another is dividing by output. That gives average cost, but the question asks for the total.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:23.259
Your turn. Now you try. A food van has fixed costs of one thousand two hundred dollars a month. Ingredients cost two dollars fifty per meal, and it sells nine hundred meals a month. Calculate total costs per month. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:37.818
Answer. Variable cost is two dollars fifty times nine hundred, which is two thousand two hundred and fifty dollars. Add one thousand two hundred: total cost is three thousand four hundred and fifty dollars.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.169
Check the picture: a small fixed cost and a larger variable cost add up to the total.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:53.071
Remember: cost per unit times quantity, add the fixed cost, and give the total with currency. Now try the practice questions.
